# env/oauth.env — the per-provider OIDC variables, and nothing else.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, when every other setting lives in the main env file.
#
# vidra-core's compose `environment:` map is an explicit allow-list: a key it
# does not name cannot reach the container, however faithfully `--env-file`
# carries it. That is deliberate everywhere else. It cannot work here, because
# the names of these variables are not knowable in advance — they are DERIVED
# from your own provider list. `OAUTH_PROVIDERS=okta` means the process looks
# for OAUTH_OKTA_ISSUER, OAUTH_OKTA_CLIENT_ID, OAUTH_OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET and
# (optionally) OAUTH_OKTA_SCOPES; `OAUTH_PROVIDERS=corp-sso` means
# OAUTH_CORP_SSO_* (dashes become underscores, everything uppercases).
#
# The A05 acceptance run measured the consequence: with all four variables in a
# filled env file, `docker compose config` delivered exactly ONE of them —
# OAUTH_PROVIDERS — and the api then refused to boot for the missing three. The
# only fix within the allow-list was to hand-edit a tracked compose file inside
# a checkout that is pinned DETACHED at a release tag.
#
# So the api and worker services read THIS file as an optional env_file. It is
# scoped to the OAUTH_<NAME>_* family on purpose: everything else stays in the
# allow-list, where an unsettable key is a bug somebody notices.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#
#   1. cp vidra-core/env/oauth.env.example vidra-core/env/oauth.env
#   2. Fill in the values below for each provider you list.
#   3. Set OAUTH_PROVIDERS in your MAIN env file (env/production.env) — that one
#      key goes through the ordinary allow-list, not this file.
#   4. Redeploy. There is nothing to enable: with no such file, compose renders
#      and boots exactly as it did before (`required: false`).
#
# NEVER COMMIT THE FILLED FILE. `env/*.env` is gitignored here for the same
# reason it is in the meta repo; only this .example is tracked. A client secret
# is a credential.
#
# FORMAT. Lines are read as literal KEY=VALUE by Compose. They are NOT compose
# substitution, so a `$` needs no escaping and quotes are not stripped the way
# a shell would strip them — write the raw value.
#
# WHAT THE SERVER REQUIRES. Every provider named in OAUTH_PROVIDERS must have
# ISSUER, CLIENT_ID and CLIENT_SECRET. An incomplete provider is a BOOT FAILURE
# with a message naming the missing variable, not a degraded login — better a
# refusal an operator reads than a sign-in button that 502s. PUBLIC_BASE_URL
# becomes REQUIRED (and https in production) as soon as the list is non-empty,
# because the redirect URI is derived from it server-side and never from a
# request parameter.
#
# WHAT TO REGISTER AT THE PROVIDER. One redirect URI, exactly:
#
#   <PUBLIC_BASE_URL>/api/v1/auth/oauth/<name>/callback
#
# …where <name> is the lowercase name as it appears in OAUTH_PROVIDERS. A
# mismatch is refused by the provider, not by vidra.
#
# A NOTE ON TRUST. A provider's word about an email address does NOT link an
# existing account here: an id_token whose address matches a local account is
# refused with `email_conflict`, and connecting a provider is something the
# account holder does from Settings › Connected logins, signed in. Configuring
# two providers therefore does not make either one a way into the other's
# accounts.
#
# --- Example: a provider named `okta` (OAUTH_PROVIDERS=okta) -----------------
# Uncomment and replace. The values below are obviously fake.
#
#OAUTH_OKTA_ISSUER=https://sso.example.org/oauth2/default
#OAUTH_OKTA_CLIENT_ID=0oaEXAMPLECLIENTID000
#OAUTH_OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-me-with-the-client-secret
# Optional. Defaults to "openid email profile"; openid is always added.
#OAUTH_OKTA_SCOPES=openid email profile
#
# --- Example: a second provider named `keycloak` -----------------------------
# OAUTH_PROVIDERS=okta,keycloak
#
#OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=https://id.example.org/realms/staff
#OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=vidra
#OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-me-with-the-client-secret
